-------------------------- HELM --------------------------------

- HELM is a package manager in Kubernetes. Just like we usually do sudo apt-get update so apt is a package manager in linux ubuntu distribution. 

- So with HELM all the common things in our manifest file will be packaged easily 

- Logically by the HELM chart, we don't need to manually write the manifest file templates for pod, deployment, service, hpa, serviceAccount, ingress etc for any resource.

----------------------------------------------------------------

--- Install HELM 

. https://helm.sh/docs/intro/install/

. curl -fsSL -o get_helm.sh https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-4
. chmod 700 get_helm.sh  // executable file
. ./get_helm.sh


- helm version
version.BuildInfo{Version:"v4.2.0", GitCommit:"06468084e85c244c712834933d25ea232a4c2093", GitTreeState:"clean", GoVersion:"go1.26.3", KubeClientVersion:"v1.36"}

---


-------------------- Creating Apache HELM chart ---------------------------- 

- helm create apache-helm
Creating apache-helm

- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm/apache-helm$ tree 
.
|-- Chart.yaml
|-- charts
|-- templates
|   |-- NOTES.txt
|   |-- _helpers.tpl
|   |-- deployment.yaml
|   |-- hpa.yaml
|   |-- httproute.yaml
|   |-- ingress.yaml
|   |-- service.yaml
|   |-- serviceaccount.yaml
|   `-- tests
|       `-- test-connection.yaml
`-- values.yaml

4 directories, 11 files

- we can configure things into the values.yaml file and those values can be injected into the local variables inside deployemnt, service etc templates file.


Now we need to package all the things which is in apache-helm dir :-
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm$ helm package apache-helm/
Successfully packaged chart and saved it to: /root/k8s-cluster/helm/apache-helm-0.1.0.tgz

After this we need to create the helm environment like for dev, sandbox and prod in the specific namespace 
- helm install dev-apache apache-helm -n dev-apache --create-namespace
Means :-
- helm install <required-chart-name> <from-exist-chart-name> -n <namespace-assign> --create-namespace

Same command for uninstall
- helm uninstall dev-apache


- kubectl get all -n dev-apache
NAME                                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
pod/dev-apache-apache-helm-6ff4f7c667-2jnfv   1/1     Running   0          4m44s

NAME                             TYPE        CLUSTER-IP     EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)   AGE
service/dev-apache-apache-helm   ClusterIP   10.96.67.189   <none>        80/TCP    4m45s

NAME                                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
deployment.apps/dev-apache-apache-helm   1/1     1            1           4m44s

NAME                                                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE
replicaset.apps/dev-apache-apache-helm-6ff4f7c667   1         1         1       4m44s

----------------------------------------------------------------------------

----------------------- Practical Usage with Helm ----------------------

--- * Go and change the replicaCount to 2 in this file :-  
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm$ vim apache-helm/values.yaml

* And then package again this file via upgrading the patch version :-  
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm$ vim apache-helm/Chart.yaml 
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm$ helm package apache-helm
Successfully packaged chart and saved it to: /root/k8s-cluster/helm/apache-helm-0.1.0.tgz
- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm$ helm upgrade prod-apache ./apache-helm -n prod-apache


--- * Rollback from this command
- helm rollback prod-apache 1 -n prod-apache
Rollback was a success! Happy Helming!

means:
prod-apache → Helm release name
1 → Revision number to roll back to
-n prod-apache → Kubernetes namespace


--------------------------------------------------------------------------

-------------------- Creating Node.js App via HELM ------------------------

- helm create node-js-app

- root@controlplane:~/k8s-cluster/helm/node-js-app$ tree
.
|-- Chart.yaml
|-- charts
|-- templates
|   |-- NOTES.txt
|   |-- _helpers.tpl
|   |-- deployment.yaml
|   |-- hpa.yaml
|   |-- httproute.yaml
|   |-- ingress.yaml
|   |-- service.yaml
|   |-- serviceaccount.yaml
|   `-- tests
|       `-- test-connection.yaml
`-- values.yaml

4 directories, 11 files

* Now change the targetPort by going into the templates service file so they can get the dynamic value from container :- 
- vim templates/service.yaml
iapiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: {{ include "node-js-app.fullname" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "node-js-app.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  type: {{ .Values.service.type }}
  ports:
    - port: {{ .Values.service.port }}
      targetPort: {{ .Values.service.targetPort }} // This one
      protocol: TCP
      name: http
  selector:
    {{- include "node-js-app.selectorLabels" . | nindent 4 }}


* Also change the required things in values.yaml file like adding ur repo image and update the tag to latest and also chanege the port, targetPort as well :- 
- vim valus.yaml
# Default values for node-js-app.
# This is a YAML-formatted file.
# Declare variables to be passed into your templates.

# This will set the replicaset count more information can be found here: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers
replicaCount: 1

# This sets the container image more information can be found here: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/
image:
  repository: hasan1702/notes-app   // This one
  # This sets the pull policy for images.
  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent
  # Overrides the image tag whose default is the chart appVersion.
  tag: "latest"   // This one

# This is for setting up a service more information can be found here: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ser
service:
  # This sets the service type more information can be found here: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service
  type: ClusterIP
  # This sets the ports more information can be found here: https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/#field
  port: 8000
  targetPort: 8000


* Now package our node-js app and also create a release from our chart
- helm package node-js-app/
- helm install dev-node-js-app node-js-app -n dev-node --create-namespace

